      "id": 1,
      "text": "In 1978, the village of Hideaway Park was incorporated and renamed Winter Park, a change influenced by Denver's mountain‑park vision.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://wpgov.com/live-play-visit/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Winter Park began as a railroad town in the early 1900s, where Doc Graves opened a drug store in 1922.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://wpgov.com/live-play-visit/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The original Hideaway Park featured ten small tourist cabins rented nightly to hunters and fishermen.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://wpgov.com/live-play-visit/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Perched on the western slope of the Continental Divide, Winter Park ranges from 8,700 to 12,060 feet above sea level.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winter_Park%2C_Colorado"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Winter Park Resort, owned by Denver and managed by Alterra, is served by Amtrak's Winter Park Express ski train.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
